# llmbellm

> Ring a terminal bell when your LLM call finishes.

LLM API calls can take a long time. `llmbellm` lets you kick off a job and walk
away, confident that your terminal will beep (or trigger a desktop notification
via a terminal bell) the moment it completes.

## Installation

```bash
pip install llmbellm
```

## Usage

### As a Python library

Wrap any callable – including an LLM client method – with `llmbellm.wrap`:

```python
import openai
from llmbellm import wrap

client = openai.OpenAI()

# Ring the bell automatically after every call
create = wrap(client.chat.completions.create, message="LLM done!")

response = create(
    model="gpt-4o",
    messages=[{"role": "user", "content": "Summarise the French Revolution."}],
)
print(response.choices[0].message.content)
```

You can also ring the bell manually at any point:

```python
from llmbellm import bell

# ... long running work ...
bell("Training finished!")
```

### As a CLI wrapper

Prefix any shell command with `llmbellm` and the bell will ring when it exits:

```bash
llmbellm python train.py
llmbellm ollama run llama3 "Tell me a joke"
```

The exit code of the wrapped command is forwarded unchanged.

## API

### `bell(message: str = "") -> None`

Write a terminal bell character (`\a`) to stderr. If *message* is given, also
print it to stdout.

### `wrap(fn, *, message="", notify_on_error=True) -> fn`

Return a wrapper around *fn* that calls `bell()` after *fn* returns (or raises).

| Parameter | Default | Description |
|-----------|---------|-------------|
| `message` | `""` | Custom message printed after the bell. Defaults to `"<fn> finished in Xs"`. |
| `notify_on_error` | `True` | Also ring the bell when *fn* raises an exception. |

